sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-11> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because a closed surface divided into a small number of faces admits only a small number of arrangements, the same few configurations of cells (and of parts and organs) recur in all kinds of plants and animals with no relation to biological classification.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Few partition patterns recur across organisms regardless of classification";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Topology of space-partitioning”. Scope: small cell groups and partitioned organs across plants and animals"
}
